 [0.6923, 30.6156]}, {\"name\": \"lagLP\", \"value\": 32.892307692307696}, {\"name\": \"lagIP\", \"value\": 25.0}, {\"name\": \"lagA\", \"value\": [7.0, 6.0]}, {\"name\": \"lagB\", \"value\": 36.0}, {\"name\": \"lagC\", \"value\": [2.0, 7.0]}, {\"name\": \"lagColors\", \"value\": {\"p1\": \"#4C72B0\", \"p2\": \"#DD8452\", \"weak\": \"#C44E52\", \"hull\": \"#55A868\", \"grid\": \"#BBBBBB\", \"text\": \"#333333\"}}]}</script>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {.callout-tip appearance=\"simple\"}\n**Interactive.** Move the price $\\lambda$. Left: the 32 points of $X$ (filled if they also\nsatisfy the relaxed constraint, dashed line) and the maximizer of the Lagrangian\nobjective (star); the arrow at the top left is the objective direction $c - \\lambda a$. Right: $L(\\lambda)$, the\nupper envelope of one line per point of $X$.\n:::\n\n```{ojs}\n//| echo: false\nviewof lam = Inputs.range([0, 1.5], {label: \"Price λ\", step: 0.01, value: 0})\n```\n\n```{ojs}\n//| echo: false\n//| column: page\nlagChart = {\n  const C = lagColors, W = 800, H = 380;\n  const vals = lagX.map((x, i) => lagIc[i] + lam * lagSl[i]);\n  const k = d3.maxIndex(vals), xs = lagX[k], Lv = vals[k];\n  const svg = d3.create(\"svg\").attr(\"viewBox\", [0, 0, W, H])\n      .style(\"width\", \"100%\").style(\"max-width\", \"960px\")\n      .style(\"font-family\", \"inherit\").style(\"font-size\", \"16px\");\n  // left: points of X\n  const x = d3.scaleLinear([-0.5, 5.5], [40, 350]), y = d3.scaleLinear([-0.5, 6.5], [340, 30]);\n  const g = svg.append(\"g\");\n  for (let t = 0; t <= 6; t++) {\n    if (t <= 5) g.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", x(t)).attr(\"y\", 362).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\")\n      .attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").attr(\"font-size\", 14).text(t);\n    g.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 24).attr(\"y\", y(t) + 5).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\")\n      .attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").attr(\"font-size\", 14).text(t);\n  }\n  g.append(\"line\").attr(\"x1\", x(0)).attr(\"y1\", y(lagB / lagA[1])).attr(\"x2\", x(lagB / lagA[0])).attr(\"y2\", y(0))\n    .attr(\"stroke\", C.p1).attr(\"stroke-width\", 2).attr(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"6 4\");\n  g.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 195).attr(\"y\", 40).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\").attr(\"fill\", C.p1)\n    .attr(\"font-size\", 13).text(\"dashed: relaxed constraint 7x₁ + 6x₂ ≤ 36\");\n  for (const p of lagX) {\n    const ok = lagA[0] * p[0] + lagA[1] * p[1] <= lagB + 1e-9;\n    g.append(\"circle\").attr(\"cx\", x(p[0])).attr(\"cy\", y(p[1])).attr(\"r\", 4)\n      .style(\"fill\", ok ? \"currentColor\" : \"var(--bs-body-bg, white)\").attr(\"stroke\", \"currentColor\");\n  }\n  const d = [lagC[0] - lam * lagA[0], lagC[1] - lam * lagA[1]], n = Math.hypot(...d) || 1;\n  svg.append(\"defs\").append(\"marker\").attr(\"id\", \"lag-arr\").attr(\"viewBox\", \"0 0 10 10\")\n    .attr(\"refX\", 9).attr(\"refY\", 5).attr(\"markerWidth\", 6).attr(\"markerHeight\", 6).attr(\"orient\", \"auto\")\n    .append(\"path\").attr(\"d\", \"M0,0L10,5L0,10z\").attr(\"fill\", C.p2);\n  const o = [0.7, 4.1];\n  g.append(\"circle\").attr(\"cx\", x(o[0])).attr(\"cy\", y(o[1])).attr(\"r\", 3).attr(\"fill\", C.p2);\n  g.append(\"line\").attr(\"x1\", x(o[0])).attr(\"y1\", y(o[1]))\n    .attr(\"x2\", x(o[0] + 0.6 * d[0] / n)).attr(\"y2\", y(o[1] + 0.6 * d[1] / n))\n    .attr(\"stroke\", C.p2).attr(\"stroke-width\", 2.5).attr(\"marker-end\", \"url(#lag-arr)\");\n  g.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", x(o[0])).attr(\"y\", y(o[1]) + 24).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\")\n    .attr(\"font-size\", 13).attr(\"fill\", C.p2).text(\"c − λa\");\n  g.append(\"path\").attr(\"d\", d3.symbol(d3.symbolStar, 260)).attr(\"transform\", `translate(${x(xs[0])},${y(xs[1])})`)\n    .attr(\"fill\", C.p2).attr(\"stroke\", \"white\");\n  g.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 195).attr(\"y\", 18).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\").attr(\"font-weight\", 600)\n    .attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").text(\"the set X\");\n  // right: L(lambda)\n  const u = d3.scaleLinear([0, 1.5], [450, 780]), v = d3.scaleLinear([20, 56], [340, 30]);\n  const r = svg.append(\"g\");\n  r.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 615).attr(\"y\", 18).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\").attr(\"font-weight\", 600)\n    .attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").text(\"Lagrangian function L(λ)\");\n  for (const t of [0, 0.5, 1, 1.5]) r.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", u(t)).attr(\"y\", 362)\n    .attr(\"text-anchor\", \"middle\").attr(\"font-size\", 14).attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").text(t);\n  for (const t of [20, 30, 40, 50]) r.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 436).attr(\"y\", v(t) + 5)\n    .attr(\"text-anchor\", \"end\").attr(\"font-size\", 14).attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").text(t);\n  r.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", 780).attr(\"y\", 380).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"end\").attr(\"font-size\", 14)\n    .attr(\"fill\", \"currentColor\").text(\"λ\");\n  const clip = svg.append(\"clipPath\").attr(\"id\", \"lag-clip\").append(\"rect\")\n    .attr(\"x\", 450).attr(\"y\", 30).attr(\"width\", 330).attr(\"height\", 310);\n  const lines = r.append(\"g\").attr(\"clip-path\", \"url(#lag-clip)\");\n  lagX.forEach((p, i) => lines.append(\"line\").attr(\"x1\", u(0)).attr(\"y1\", v(lagIc[i]))\n    .attr(\"x2\", u(1.5)).attr(\"y2\", v(lagIc[i] + 1.5 * lagSl[i]))\n    .attr(\"stroke\", i === k ? C.p2 : \"currentColor\").attr(\"stroke-opacity\", i === k ? 0.9 : 0.12)\n    .attr(\"stroke-width\", i === k ? 2 : 1));\n  for (const [val, col, lab] of [[lagLP, C.weak, \"LP relaxation\"], [lagIP, C.hull, \"integer optimum\"]]) {\n    lines.append(\"line\").attr(\"x1\", u(0)).attr(\"x2\", u(1.5)).attr(\"y1\", v(val)).attr(\"y2\", v(val))\n      .attr(\"stroke\", col).attr(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"5 4\").attr(\"stroke-width\", 1.6);\n    r.append(\"text\").attr(\"x\", u(1.5) - 4).attr(\"y\", v(val) - 6).attr(\"text-anchor\", \"end\")\n      .attr(\"font-size\", 13).attr(\"fill\", col).text(`${lab} ${val.toFixed(1)}`);\n  }\n  lines.append(\"path\").attr(\"d\", d3.line(p => u(p[0]), p => v(p[1]))(lagCurve))\n    .attr(\"fill\", \"none\").attr(\"stroke\", C.p1).attr(\"stroke-width\", 3);\n  r.append(\"circle\").attr(\"cx\", u(lagStar[0])).attr(\"cy\", v(lagStar[1])).attr(\"r\", 5).attr(\"fill\", C.p1);\n  r.append(\"circle\").attr(\"cx\", u(lam)).attr(\"cy\", v(Lv)).attr(\"r\", 7).attr(\"fill\", C.p2).attr(\"stroke\", \"white\");\n  return svg.node();\n}\n```\n\n```{ojs}\n//| echo: false\nlagText = {\n  const vals = lagX.map((x, i) => lagIc[i] + lam * lagSl[i]);\n  const k = d3.maxIndex(vals), xs = lagX[k], slack = lagB - lagA[0] * xs[0] - lagA[1] * xs[1];\n  const box = document.createElement(\"div\"); box.style.minHeight = \"4em\";\n  const status = slack < 0\n    ? `It violates the relaxed constraint by ${-slack}, so a higher price would help: L decreases as λ grows.`\n    : slack > 0 ? `It satisfies the relaxed constraint with slack ${slack}, so the price is too high: L decreases as λ shrinks.`\n    : `It satisfies the relaxed constraint with equality.`;\n  box.innerHTML = `<p>λ = ${lam.toFixed(2)}: the maximizer is x = (${xs.join(\", \")}) and\n    L(λ) = ${(lagIc[k]).toFixed(0)} + ${lam.toFixed(2)} × (${lagSl[k]}) = <b>${vals[k].toFixed(2)}</b>.\n    ${status}</p>\n    <p>The minimum, <b>${lagStar[1].toFixed(2)}</b> at λ ≈ ${lagStar[0].toFixed(2)}, lies between the\n    LP bound ${lagLP.toFixed(2)} and the integer optimum ${lagIP}.</p>`;\n  return box;\n}\n```\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n## Static version of this figure (for printing)\n\n::: {#cell-fig-lagrangian .cell execution_count=3}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![The Lagrangian function of the running example (blue): the upper envelope of 32 lines, one per point of $X$ (grey). Its minimum, about 30.6 at $\\lambda \\approx 0.69$, is the Lagrangian bound. It improves on the LP bound 32.9 (red) but does not reach the integer optimum 25 (green): there is a duality gap.](lagrangian-relaxation_files/figure-html/fig-lagrangian-output-1.png){#fig-lagrangian width=592 height=418}\n:::\n:::\n\n\n:::\n\n## How good is the bound? Geoffrion's theorem\n\n::: {.callout-important}\n## Theorem (Geoffrion)\n$$\nz_{\\text{LD}} = \\max\\{c^\\top x : Ax \\le b,\\ x \\in \\operatorname{conv}(X)\\}.\n$$\n:::\n\nSo the Lagrangian dual convexifies **only the kept constraints**: it takes the integer\nhull of $X$, intersects it with the relaxed constraints as they are, and solves that LP.\nTwo consequences:\n\n- $z_{\\text{IP}} \\le z_{\\text{LD}} \\le z_{\\text{LP}}$. The Lagrangian bound is never worse\n  than the LP bound, because $\\operatorname{conv}(X) \\subseteq \\{x : Dx \\le d\\}$.\n- If $\\{x : Dx \\le d\\}$ already has integer vertices (Geoffrion's **integrality\n  property**), then $\\operatorname{conv}(X) = \\{x : Dx \\le d\\}$ and\n  $z_{\\text{LD}} = z_{\\text{LP}}$: the relaxation buys nothing in bound quality, only in\n  speed. To gain, the kept problem must be \"hard enough\" not to be solved by its own LP.\n\n::: {#cell-fig-geoffrion .cell execution_count=4}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![Geoffrion's theorem on the running example. The kept set $X$ (dots) has convex hull conv$(X)$ (blue). Intersecting it with the relaxed constraint $7x_1 + 6x_2 \\le 36$ (dashed) gives the orange region, and its LP optimum (orange star) has value 30.6, exactly the Lagrangian bound. The LP relaxation (red outline) is larger, with optimum 32.9; the integer hull (green) is smaller, with optimum 25.](lagrangian-relaxation_files/figure-html/fig-geoffrion-output-1.png){#fig-geoffrion width=348 height=477}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn the example, $\\operatorname{conv}(X)$ adds exactly one inequality to the kept\nconstraints, $-x_1 + x_2 \\le 2$ (the first Chvátal–Gomory cut on the\n[cutting planes](cutting-planes.qmd) page), and the Lagrangian bound equals the LP bound\nafter that cut, $30.62$.\n\n::: {.callout-note collapse=\"true\"}\n## Proof sketch of Geoffrion's theorem\nSince a linear function has the same maximum over $X$ as over $\\operatorname{conv}(X)$,\n$L(\\lambda) = \\max\\{c^\\top x + \\lambda^\\top(b - Ax) : x \\in \\operatorname{conv}(X)\\}$.\nWith $X$ bounded, $\\operatorname{conv}(X) = \\{x : Fx \\le f\\}$ is a polytope, so this is an\nLP. Write its dual and combine it with the outer minimization over $\\lambda \\ge 0$:\n$$\n\\min_{\\lambda \\ge 0} \\max_{Fx \\le f}\\ c^\\top x + \\lambda^\\top (b - Ax)\n\\;=\\; \\max\\{c^\\top x : Ax \\le b,\\ Fx \\le f\\}\n$$\nby [LP duality](../../linear-programming/duality/lp-duality.qmd) applied to the LP on the\nright, with $\\lambda$ the duals of $Ax \\le b$.\n:::\n\n## Solving the Lagrangian dual: the subgradient method\n\n$L$ is convex but not differentiable at its kinks. If $x^\\lambda$ is a maximizer at\n$\\lambda$, then $g = b - Ax^\\lambda$ is a **subgradient**: $L(\\mu) \\ge L(\\lambda) +\ng^\\top(\\mu - \\lambda)$ for all $\\mu$. The **subgradient method** steps against it and\nprojects back onto $\\lambda \\ge 0$:\n$$\n\\lambda^{k+1} = \\max\\{0,\\ \\lambda^k - t_k\\, (b - Ax^{\\lambda^k})\\}.\n$$\nIntuitively: if the relaxed constraint is violated, raise its price; if it has slack,\nlower the price. With step sizes $t_k \\to 0$ and $\\sum_k t_k = \\infty$ the best value\nfound converges to $z_{\\text{LD}}$. @held1974 tested the method on large problems and\nproposed step sizes based on an estimate of the optimal value.\n\n::: {#cell-fig-subgradient .cell execution_count=5}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}\n![Thirty subgradient iterations from $\\lambda = 0$ with steps $t_k = 0.005/\\sqrt{k+1}$. The price (left) climbs while the relaxed constraint is violated, then zigzags around $\\lambda^\\ast \\approx 0.69$; the bound (right) is not monotone, but the best value so far (dark line) approaches the Lagrangian bound 30.6 (dashed).](lagrangian-relaxation_files/figure-html/fig-subgradient-output-1.png){#fig-subgradient width=974 height=348}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe method is simple and needs only an oracle for the easy problem, which is why it is\npopular for large structured problems. Its drawbacks are slow, non-monotone convergence\nand no primal solution: the maximizers $x^{\\lambda^k}$ are usually infeasible, and a\nheuristic must repair them into a feasible solution (which gives a lower bound, so the\ngap can be monitored). The LP-based view of\n[Dantzig–Wolfe decomposition](../../decomposition-methods/dantzig-wolfe/index.qmd) with\ncolumn generation, solves the same dual more robustly, and also produces primal information.\n\n## In practice\n\n- **Choose what to relax** so that the remaining problem is easy but does not have the\n  integrality property. The classic examples relax constraints that couple otherwise\n  independent pieces (one knapsack, shortest path or spanning tree per piece).\n- **Inside branch and bound**, the Lagrangian bound replaces the LP bound at each node,\n  which pays off when it is much tighter or much cheaper to compute.\n- **Lagrangian heuristics** round or repair $x^{\\lambda}$ to feasible solutions,\n  often of good quality, since the prices push toward feasibility.\n\n## Further reading\n\n- @geoffrion1974 for the theory, and @fisher1981 for a readable tutorial.\n- @held1970 for the original application to the travelling-salesman problem.\n- @conforti2014 for Lagrangian duality and its link to Dantzig–Wolfe reformulation.\n\n",
